1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,768
This tutorial covers the money going out:

2
00:00:02,768 --> 00:00:05,536
supplier bills, paying them, and supplier
cheques.

3
00:00:05,536 --> 00:00:08,699
As the Accountant, open Receipt and
Payment Vouchers.

4
00:00:08,699 --> 00:00:13,444
Every supplier bill and every payment of
the year is listed here.

5
00:00:13,444 --> 00:00:16,607
Create a Purchase Invoice from BlueWave
Cleaning for

6
00:00:16,607 --> 00:00:19,375
September's common-area cleaning at Palm
Ridge Residences.

7
00:00:19,375 --> 00:00:21,748
Enter BlueWave's invoice number and date.

8
00:00:21,748 --> 00:00:24,516
The due date follows their 30-day terms.

9
00:00:24,516 --> 00:00:28,074
The line goes to Cleaning Services: 2,400,
plus 5

10
00:00:28,074 --> 00:00:31,238
percent VAT of 120, a total of 2,520.

11
00:00:31,238 --> 00:00:35,192
Post it, then open its journal from the
voucher list.

12
00:00:35,192 --> 00:00:39,937
The purchase invoice journal debits
Cleaning Services with 2,400 and Input VAT

13
00:00:39,937 --> 00:00:44,286
with 120, and credits BlueWave with 2,520,
the amount you owe.

14
00:00:44,286 --> 00:00:48,636
Input VAT is VAT you can reclaim on your
VAT return.

15
00:00:48,636 --> 00:00:50,218
Now open Payables aging.

16
00:00:50,218 --> 00:00:54,567
BlueWave's 2,520 sits in Current, because
it is not due yet.

17
00:00:54,567 --> 00:00:59,312
The last column checks that each
supplier's open invoices add up to

18
00:00:59,312 --> 00:01:03,662
their balance in the ledger, and here the
difference is zero.

19
00:01:03,662 --> 00:01:07,616
To pay, open Payment runs and start a new
run.

20
00:01:07,616 --> 00:01:11,965
List what is due by 31 October and tick
BlueWave's invoice.

21
00:01:11,965 --> 00:01:14,733
Pay by transfer on 30 September, from

22
00:01:14,733 --> 00:01:17,501
the Emirates Islamic account for the
Residences.

23
00:01:17,501 --> 00:01:20,665
The preview shows the payment voucher
before anything

24
00:01:20,665 --> 00:01:23,828
is written: BlueWave debited, the bank
credited, 2,520.

25
00:01:23,828 --> 00:01:27,387
It also warns that BlueWave has no bank
account

26
00:01:27,387 --> 00:01:30,550
number on file for the bank upload file.

27
00:01:30,550 --> 00:01:31,736
Post the run.

28
00:01:31,736 --> 00:01:34,504
Each supplier gets one payment voucher,
and

29
00:01:34,504 --> 00:01:36,877
if anything fails, nothing is posted.

30
00:01:36,877 --> 00:01:40,435
When you pay by a cheque dated later, the

31
00:01:40,435 --> 00:01:43,994
bank has not paid yet, so the voucher
credits

32
00:01:43,994 --> 00:01:47,157
PDC Payable, issued cheques, instead of
the bank.

33
00:01:47,157 --> 00:01:48,344
Open Issued cheques.

34
00:01:48,344 --> 00:01:51,902
BlueWave's August cheque, 004501, was
presented on 12 September.

35
00:01:51,902 --> 00:01:55,461
Presenting it posted a journal that debits
PDC Payable

36
00:01:55,461 --> 00:01:59,020
and credits the bank: the bank balance
falls when

37
00:01:59,020 --> 00:02:02,579
the bank pays, not when you write the
cheque.

38
00:02:02,579 --> 00:02:06,137
Cheque 004521 is dated 20 October and
still outstanding,

39
00:02:06,137 --> 00:02:09,696
and the outstanding total equals the PDC
Payable balance.
